#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/id #uuid "3344e23f-56eb-5880-b1cd-7198525daf91" :question/math-academy-id "e-12554" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nz^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\nz &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\ge 0; \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\le 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 = 1\n$$\n\nand its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{(x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq 1 \\bigg\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124226 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/id #uuid "ca62112b-9989-5ede-9cbf-561f5dc2ba43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5263" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} + {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + (0)^{2} + ((0 + 1)^{2})/(2) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 2 ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are\n\n$$\n\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 0, 0\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "8db4fc0c-416b-57a4-8612-a422a7681076" :question/math-academy-id "e-5311" :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 8z &= 20 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z^{2} + 8z + 16) &= 20 + 16 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 4)^{2} &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/id #uuid "ecc934f5-9339-5076-b2d0-58b113364b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-5312" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + (0 - 3)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + 9 &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :